WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.850
Listener: What does checkout need from the
rest of the shop?

00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:07.175
Guide: The web service calls the API.

00:00:07.175 --> 00:00:09.700
The API calls the database service,

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:13.700
which uses a PostgreSQL server named
pg-shop.

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:18.175
Follow those arrows from checkout and you
can see which calls have to succeed.

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:22.375
Listener: Where did the rest of those dots
come from?

00:00:23.075 --> 00:00:26.450
Guide: Kubernetes records say where the
shop’s pods ran.

00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:29.350
Network records say which pods called each
other.

00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:30.700
By the end of the replay,

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:35.450
Sattle’s master graph holds seventy-three
entities and sixty-nine links.

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:38.325
Each dot stands for something a source
reported.

00:00:39.967 --> 00:00:42.642
Listener: How does that map become a
health model?

00:00:43.367 --> 00:00:46.317
Guide: Start with the services checkout
depends on.

00:00:46.317 --> 00:00:49.917
A query groups the underlying records into
those services

00:00:49.917 --> 00:00:52.092
and keeps the calls between them.

00:00:52.092 --> 00:00:54.742
That transformation is a projection.

00:00:54.742 --> 00:00:57.717
A health projection also uses readings and
rules

00:00:57.717 --> 00:01:00.492
to work out which services a failure
affects.

00:01:02.575 --> 00:01:05.825
Listener: Why is the database server
outside the box?

00:01:06.525 --> 00:01:09.850
Guide: The box marks the shop’s Kubernetes
namespace.

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:12.875
The database service belongs to that
namespace.

00:01:12.875 --> 00:01:16.150
Its PostgreSQL server runs on Azure.

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:19.450
The boundary tells Sattle which services
to start with.

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:22.275
Following a dependency can take it
outside.

00:01:23.925 --> 00:01:28.550
Listener: How long does the database have
to go quiet before an alarm opens?

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:32.550
Guide: One bad minute opens the pg-shop
alarm.

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:35.400
It takes two good minutes in a row to
close it.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.650
The rule remembers the previous minute.

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:39.500
That’s called hysteresis.

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:42.475
A team can choose these thresholds for its
service.

00:01:44.108 --> 00:01:46.833
Listener: Could a failed call leave
checkout working?

00:01:47.533 --> 00:01:52.833
Guide: Checkout needs the database, so the
database alarm counts against the API.

00:01:52.833 --> 00:01:55.658
For a service that can carry on without
its cache,

00:01:55.658 --> 00:01:58.733
a rule could treat the cache call as
optional.

00:01:58.733 --> 00:02:01.608
Network records establish that a call
happened.

00:02:01.608 --> 00:02:03.858
A rule says what its failure means.

00:02:05.858 --> 00:02:09.233
Listener: How does Sattle know which
server the meter belongs to?

00:02:10.008 --> 00:02:13.283
Guide: Azure Resource Graph lists pg-shop.

00:02:13.283 --> 00:02:17.483
Azure Monitor puts the very same resource
ID on the reading.

00:02:17.483 --> 00:02:19.958
Sattle can use that ID to attach the
reading

00:02:19.958 --> 00:02:23.483
to the right server, even when different
sources describe it.

00:02:25.133 --> 00:02:28.958
Listener: Why is there just one arrow
between web and API?

00:02:29.708 --> 00:02:32.733
Guide: Three web pods call two API pods.

00:02:32.733 --> 00:02:35.683
Pods are running copies of an application.

00:02:35.683 --> 00:02:41.108
Group the pods by service and their calls
become one link from web to API.

00:02:41.108 --> 00:02:44.733
The records underneath still show which
copies made the calls.

00:02:46.383 --> 00:02:50.883
Listener: What does the arrow from the
database service to pg-shop mean?

00:02:51.583 --> 00:02:54.383
Guide: Pg-shop backs the database service.

00:02:54.383 --> 00:02:58.008
That’s a different relationship from one
pod calling another.

00:02:58.008 --> 00:03:02.908
Sattle’s ontology names the kinds of
things and links the model understands.

00:03:02.908 --> 00:03:07.333
A team can maintain that vocabulary as new
kinds of software appear.

00:03:09.358 --> 00:03:12.033
Listener: The reading arrived after ten
twelve.

00:03:12.033 --> 00:03:14.683
Why does the chart put it at ten eleven?

00:03:15.458 --> 00:03:18.608
Guide: Azure Monitor measured a zero at
ten eleven.

00:03:18.608 --> 00:03:21.758
The recorder received it a minute and a
second later.

00:03:21.758 --> 00:03:23.908
The chart uses the sample time,

00:03:23.908 --> 00:03:26.883
so the failure lands in the minute the
meter observed it.

00:03:28.508 --> 00:03:31.458
Listener: Why have web and the gateway
turned red too?

00:03:32.183 --> 00:03:34.083
Guide: Both call the API.

00:03:34.083 --> 00:03:38.933
The API needs the database service, which
depends on pg-shop.

00:03:38.933 --> 00:03:42.808
The model carries the database alarm back
along those links.

00:03:42.808 --> 00:03:46.933
The cache stays green because its own work
doesn’t depend on that server.

00:03:48.617 --> 00:03:51.592
Listener: At ten fourteen the database
answers again.

00:03:51.592 --> 00:03:53.292
Why is it still red?

00:03:54.017 --> 00:03:56.667
Guide: That’s the first good minute after
the outage.

00:03:56.667 --> 00:04:00.317
At ten fifteen the second good reading
clears the alarm.

00:04:00.317 --> 00:04:02.367
Its callers recover too.

00:04:02.367 --> 00:04:04.542
The meter shows the latest sample.

00:04:04.542 --> 00:04:07.642
The alarm also remembers what happened a
minute ago.

00:04:09.675 --> 00:04:12.500
Listener: What was that amber patch
earlier in the morning?

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:16.625
Guide: The cache was under load, and web
already relied on it.

00:04:16.625 --> 00:04:19.425
Both show Degraded, the warning state.

00:04:19.425 --> 00:04:23.225
In that same snapshot the API and gateway
are healthy.

00:04:23.225 --> 00:04:25.300
The cache warning hasn’t reached them.

00:04:26.925 --> 00:04:31.050
Listener: Where did the new arrow from the
API to the cache come from?

00:04:31.775 --> 00:04:34.350
Guide: In the second run, the API’s
deployment

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:37.025
has a setting that names the cache host.

00:04:37.025 --> 00:04:40.600
Sattle matches that address to the
existing Azure cache.

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:44.325
The deployment setting is the evidence for
the new dependency.

00:04:45.967 --> 00:04:49.442
Listener: Why does the gateway change
color in only one run?

00:04:50.167 --> 00:04:55.042
Guide: Both runs stop at the same moment
with cache load at eighty-five percent.

00:04:55.042 --> 00:04:58.092
In the second run the API depends on the
cache,

00:04:58.092 --> 00:05:00.667
and the gateway depends on the API.

00:05:00.667 --> 00:05:03.242
The new link carries the warning to the
gateway.

00:05:05.275 --> 00:05:08.425
Listener: The API now points at
offers-cache.

00:05:08.425 --> 00:05:10.825
Is that enough to put the cache on the
map?

00:05:11.525 --> 00:05:13.650
Guide: The deployment gives a host name.

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:18.475
At this point in the replay, Azure
Resource Graph has no matching resource.

00:05:18.475 --> 00:05:22.050
The setting tells us where the API expects
to connect.

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:25.050
The resource behind that address is still
unknown.

00:05:26.650 --> 00:05:29.000
Listener: Which record settles the
question?

00:05:29.775 --> 00:05:34.500
Guide: Azure Resource Graph returns a
Redis resource named offers-cache.

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:37.500
Its name matches the first part of the
address.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:41.050
Its resource type matches the Redis host
suffix.

00:05:41.050 --> 00:05:44.200
The query can now link the API to that
cache.

00:05:45.867 --> 00:05:48.817
Listener: Why is there a question mark on
the new cache?

00:05:49.517 --> 00:05:52.192
Guide: The resource record identifies the
cache,

00:05:52.192 --> 00:05:56.492
but the replay has no reading that tells
the health rule how it’s doing.

00:05:56.492 --> 00:05:58.842
Sattle marks its state Unknown.

00:05:58.842 --> 00:06:00.717
To judge a different kind of resource,

00:06:00.717 --> 00:06:03.517
the model may need a different meter or
rule.

00:06:05.525 --> 00:06:09.775
Listener: Does the Canvas download the
whole map when the database fails?

00:06:10.525 --> 00:06:12.950
Guide: It already has revision fifteen.

00:06:12.950 --> 00:06:15.675
Seven edits take it to revision sixteen.

00:06:15.675 --> 00:06:18.900
The engine sends those edits together as a
patch.

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:21.675
The Canvas can apply the patch to the map
it has.

00:06:23.275 --> 00:06:26.700
Listener: How far apart are revisions
sixteen and eighteen?

00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:30.500
Guide: In sixteen, pg-shop is unhealthy.

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:32.600
By eighteen it has recovered.

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:35.275
Those numbers count changes to the graph.

00:06:35.275 --> 00:06:39.750
To find the elapsed time, look at the
timestamps on the database readings.

00:06:41.383 --> 00:06:44.658
Listener: How can we check what the map
showed during the outage?

00:06:45.408 --> 00:06:49.383
Guide: Replay the shop records and clock
events with the same rules.

00:06:49.383 --> 00:06:52.933
Applying each update as it arrives gives
the same graph

00:06:52.933 --> 00:06:56.333
as computing the answer again from the
recorded inputs.

00:06:56.333 --> 00:06:58.683
That gives us a way to check the result.

00:07:00.742 --> 00:07:01.867
Listener: Web is red.

00:07:01.867 --> 00:07:03.567
Where did the failure start?

00:07:04.317 --> 00:07:07.617
Guide: In revision sixteen, follow web
through the API

00:07:07.617 --> 00:07:10.367
and database service to pg-shop.

00:07:10.367 --> 00:07:13.167
The server’s own meter tripped its alarm.

00:07:13.167 --> 00:07:16.167
The Canvas marks pg-shop as the origin,

00:07:16.167 --> 00:07:20.042
so you can tell where the failure began
and which services felt it.

00:07:21.650 --> 00:07:26.000
Listener: Can we check the link between
the database service and pg-shop?

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:29.925
Guide: The structure query matched the
service’s configured host

00:07:29.925 --> 00:07:31.575
to the Azure resource.

00:07:31.575 --> 00:07:34.800
The health view uses the link that query
produced.

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:37.000
Following a result back through its
queries

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:39.700
to the source records is called lineage.

00:07:41.317 --> 00:07:44.392
Listener: Could we try a different rule on
this same outage?

00:07:45.092 --> 00:07:48.642
Guide: The Canvas can run a draft query
against the recorded shop

00:07:48.642 --> 00:07:50.917
and show the rows it returns.

00:07:50.917 --> 00:07:54.317
That gives a team a way to try a rule on a
known failure

00:07:54.317 --> 00:07:56.867
and inspect the result before adopting it.
